WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.229 --> 00:00:04.480
We are taking your phone calls at six one
seven eight three zero forty seven fifty.

00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:09.150
Let me pick up on our caller from the five
five nine areaCode who's calling today from

00:00:09.150 --> 00:00:10.269
555 nine.

00:00:10.269 --> 00:00:13.039
Hi, David, this is John.

00:00:13.039 --> 00:00:15.259
Hey, John, what's up?

00:00:15.259 --> 00:00:16.259
Hey.

00:00:16.259 --> 00:00:21.460
So I was wondering, what do you think the
likelihood of the next president, either Democratic

00:00:21.460 --> 00:00:25.730
or Republican, you know, whoever they may
be with, that they would create some sort

00:00:25.730 --> 00:00:35.590
of federal agency to oversee police investigations
into police violence and misconduct like that?

00:00:35.590 --> 00:00:40.800
And what do you think the effectiveness of
such a agency could be?

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:45.649
So I don't know if we need a federal agency
to oversee police.

00:00:45.649 --> 00:00:49.510
I think it would be really tough to get that
done with police unions being as strong as

00:00:49.510 --> 00:00:51.379
they are in the next segment.

00:00:51.379 --> 00:00:55.590
I am going to give my prescription for dealing
with police violence and I'm going to talk

00:00:55.590 --> 00:00:57.809
about databases of sorts.

00:00:57.809 --> 00:01:02.260
I think that we need two things and I won't
fully go into them because I'm going to do

00:01:02.260 --> 00:01:03.289
this next segment.

00:01:03.289 --> 00:01:08.830
Number one, there should be a national database
of bad actors in police departments because

00:01:08.830 --> 00:01:10.440
right now it is too easy.

00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:17.120
I'm not saying it's easy, but it is too easy
for a police officer to make a deal to leave

00:01:17.120 --> 00:01:20.050
a department because of wrongdoing or complaints.

00:01:20.050 --> 00:01:25.110
But it's done in a way where there's no place
that future police departments can see this

00:01:25.110 --> 00:01:29.270
officers history and they end up rehiring
a problem officer.

00:01:29.270 --> 00:01:33.600
And the vast majority of police officers are
not the problem.

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:36.690
The small percentage there are often are repeat
offenders.

00:01:36.690 --> 00:01:40.420
So by just eliminating a few of these bad
actors, you would dramatically reduce the

00:01:40.420 --> 00:01:42.750
number of bad acts, so to speak.

00:01:42.750 --> 00:01:50.560
So you establish a database of police officer
complaints and you require both that a police

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:56.070
department has to report to the database and
a new hiring police department must check

00:01:56.070 --> 00:02:00.940
the database so that nobody can get away with
saying we just didn't know.

00:02:00.940 --> 00:02:02.250
So that's one thing.

00:02:02.250 --> 00:02:07.240
The other thing is, I think that we do need
a review system for police shootings and use

00:02:07.240 --> 00:02:13.299
of force incidents that includes civilians,
where in many cases these things are reviewed

00:02:13.299 --> 00:02:19.950
essentially internally, internally by police,
sometimes in concert with, you know, the d.a.'s

00:02:19.950 --> 00:02:21.430
office in certain situations.

00:02:21.430 --> 00:02:22.940
I don't think that that's cutting it.

00:02:22.940 --> 00:02:27.530
I think we definitely need accountability
to civilians as well.

00:02:27.530 --> 00:02:29.750
So those are a couple of the things I think
we need to do.

00:02:29.750 --> 00:02:33.890
I think a federal agency to do the reviewing
is going to be a tough sell.

00:02:33.890 --> 00:02:40.069
Yeah, I was thinking that being like implicating
such a system would probably, if anything,

00:02:40.069 --> 00:02:47.019
does cause a lot of backlash and you know
that attention to the police union and stuff

00:02:47.019 --> 00:02:48.099
like that.

00:02:48.099 --> 00:02:49.700
But I had one other quick question.

00:02:49.700 --> 00:02:50.700
Yeah.

00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:54.689
About Trump and his Twitter account there
is trying to push for the new restrictions

00:02:54.689 --> 00:03:03.870
on them, silencing conservative voices and
whenever making it easier to not get blocked,

00:03:03.870 --> 00:03:06.139
you know, to be conservative or whatever.

00:03:06.139 --> 00:03:07.139
Right.

00:03:07.139 --> 00:03:13.799
So is that in order to helps stop Twitter
from blocking him the day he's no longer president?

00:03:13.799 --> 00:03:16.920
I don't think that he's thinking ahead in
that way whatsoever.

00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:21.280
I think that these are basically politically
partizan things that Trump believes now will

00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:22.859
benefit his side.

00:03:22.859 --> 00:03:26.360
I don't think he's saying I'm going to do
this so that Twitter doesn't block me the

00:03:26.360 --> 00:03:27.360
day I'm out.

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:29.900
I just don't think he's thinking about that.

00:03:29.900 --> 00:03:30.900
I honestly, I don't.

00:03:30.900 --> 00:03:32.579
I really don't.

00:03:32.579 --> 00:03:38.342
Well, maybe he's just incapable of looking
that far forward.

00:03:38.342 --> 00:03:39.420
But one of the reasons why.

00:03:39.420 --> 00:03:40.420
All right.

00:03:40.420 --> 00:03:41.420
Thank you, my friend.

00:03:41.420 --> 00:03:42.420
Appreciate the phone call.

00:03:42.420 --> 00:03:43.169
Great to hear from you.

